  "metadata": {
    "social-codes-pcl": {
      "title": "Social Codes",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Casey REAS",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x457ee5f723c7606c12a7264b52e285906f91eea6",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Casey%20REAS"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "For twenty-five years, I’ve collaborated with artists who write code. For us, the internet is more than a space for documentation; it is our primary venue for exhibition, discourse, and community. *Social Codes* offers a window into this hybrid community of artist-coders in 2021.\n\n*Social Codes* is an exhibition of software, or generative, art. Each piece is native digital work—a performance of code choreographed by the artist. While some works draw on the histories of drawing, painting, animation, and video, they exist fundamentally as software.\n\nArtists have worked with code since the 1960s, evolving from languages designed for room-sized computers to artist-created tools that run within a web browser. Today, each programming language functions as a distinct material. \n\nThis exhibition began in September 2020 with a crowdsourced inquiry on social media, which generated a <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1bSg5QzNrVtRUjYUSQQ4r6L25xrgUZevGdFMnuwCmMFc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">public document</a> of over three hundred artists. *Social Codes* brings these works together in an intentional space, rather than leaving them scattered across disparate platforms. \n\nThe majority of the work here relates to the coding platform Processing and the JavaScript library p5.js. As a co-founder of Processing, my perspective is shaped by this specific community; this exhibition does not represent the entirety of the field. If this is your first encounter with software art, I hope it serves as an invitation to explore the decades of work available through your browser.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/828f2aa7-7edd-4961-f5c7-0fbac8a9ec00/raw"
    },
    "the-bardo-unpacking-the-real-a2j": {
      "title": "The Bardo: Unpacking the Real",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Julie Walsh",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x8a4ee8403095bb3bbdeaed1b1cab0dcb802ae154",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Julie%20Walsh"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "*The Bardo: Unpacking the Real* is an international exhibition featuring artists from Vienna, Rome, Los Angeles, and New York that explores the ephemeral nature of sculptural art in the digital age. The exhibit examines the real and (un)real in digital sculpture, the act of collecting digital works, and the role of Bitmark NFTs in the creative process.\n\nThe exhibition title draws from the Tibetan concept of the \"Bardo\"—a liminal state of being between death and rebirth. As my mother often said, \"We are always in a Bardo,\" or a state of becoming. This concept mirrors the flux of digital sculpture, where the medium itself is in constant transition. \n\nInitially conceived as a standard digital sculpture show, the project underwent a conceptual reboot in response to the hype surrounding NFTs. We shifted our focus to treat NFTs not merely as a sales mechanism, but as a medium—much like a site-specific installation where Feral File serves as the site. Drawing on Kant’s \"thing in itself,\" the exhibition challenges our ability to categorize the immaterial as the real becomes increasingly (un)real through digital reinterpretation.\n\nThe participating artists reinvent how sculpture is seen and collected. Works range from desktop applications and AR/VR experiences to 3D scans merging physical objects with sculpted forms. We also explore the nature of ownership: how does one own the ephemeral? The exhibition presents various solutions, from password-protected viewing to the privilege of repurposing files, granting the digital work a new life. \n\n*The Bardo: Unpacking the Real* captures an exhilarating moment in art history, reflecting the human and electromagnetic energy of a medium in constant flux.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/41ccf21d-e60e-4257-97f0-c71260113d00/raw"
    },
    "fragments-of-a-hologram-rose-liz": {
      "title": "Fragments of a Hologram Rose",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Rick Silva",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x3585bd49f694aef27c9438dd91a53dda1d83eda2",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Rick%20Silva"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "This exhibition borrows its name from Willliam Gibson&rsquo;s first published short story, &ldquo;Fragments of a Hologram Rose&rdquo; (1977). As the story notes, when a hologram is cut into pieces, even the smallest section still contains the whole of the image. In a similar way, plant cuttings and an edition of digital works can both hold these mirroring elements of origin and multiplicity.\n\nThe artists featured in the exhibition were asked to imagine and create their own sci-fi plants, and to interpret this prompt however they like&mdash;whether it be by rendering an alien pollen, some photosynthetic architecture, or a flower whose weirdness is only revealed by its description. I invited these particular artists to this exhibition because their work already pointed towards these speculative notions of nature and time. They were also invited because of their experimental use, and creative misuse, of 3D modeling and animation. A 3D program&rsquo;s workspace is by default an infinite space, since as you navigate the viewport you are surrounded by a void on all sides&mdash;beyond which a sublime vastness awaits. In this way, the artists who choose to work in these programs are negotiating the infinite potentials of space and time whenever they start a new project.\n\nLike botanists from a parallel future or an unknown world, these artists cultivate their fictions into existence. They sub-scatter seed pods, prune polygon cuttings, give their models plenty of volumetric lighting, and soak with fluid simulations. The 3D objects in this exhibition have secret pathways and telepathic tendrils. They bloom on neon skins and disperse nano mold spores through LCD screens. Like an ikebana of poisonous flowers in zero gravity, they hover in the expanse as a collection of fragmented flora files.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/6a6515ed-f6c1-4942-78c2-e75d86604d00/raw"
    },
    "the-long-cut-bqv": {
      "title": "The Long Cut",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Iris Long",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x5eeff96628b5bebe4c28a53d724fb4ec3c011689",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Iris%20Long"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "A dialogue between a \"Progress-tracer\" and a \"Topology-mancer\" frames the exhibition *The Long Cut*. The former, trapped in a digital architecture built from rigid, invalidated blueprints, seeks an escape. The latter, an indigenous inhabitant of this complex, non-linear space, cannot provide a map but offers a different way of being. Through their aimless, organic wandering, the Progress-tracer realizes that the world has evolved into a topological version of its original design, where shortcuts are obsolete and the only way to \"figure it out\" is to explore without a map.\n\n*The Long Cut* reimagines the \"shortcut\"—a mechanism for accelerated time and strategic efficiency that has trained us to abandon stillness and meandering. In a world defined by global uncertainty, the exhibition invites us to develop an alternative blueprint that rejects the pursuit of the shortcut. \n\nFeaturing works that investigate the remote, latent, and liminal spaces of technology, the exhibition explores how artists navigate the hybridity of the physical and digital. By balancing the perspectives of the Progress-tracer and the Topology-mancer, the artists propose alternative pathways and maps for our shared technological reality. \n\n*Inspired by Franco \"Bifo\" Berardi’s \"Breathing: Chaos and Poetry,\" which examines the relationship between the acceleration of cyberspace and the rhythm of mental time.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/007fab41-3be7-4d30-1d58-adf8051d5700/raw"
    },
    "instructions-follow-f1c": {
      "title": "Instructions Follow",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Pau Waelder",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0xee42df737fdb9ce7e30b4cbc368e0a187ff689b2",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Pau%20Waelder"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "Over the last year, our environment has taken the shape of a video game tutorial: security tape, directional arrows, and protocols define our movement. Simultaneously, synthetic voices from our devices provide constant instructions, nudging us to meditate, exercise, or learn. We are increasingly conditioned to follow these cues—not only from authorities but from the algorithms that customize our experiences and influence our behavior.\n\n*Instructions Follow* explores our relationship with digital devices and information systems, reflecting on how our present is governed by codes, regulations, and defaults.\n\nLauren Lee McCarthy and Thierry Fournier create experiences of otherness, confronting viewers with digital clones or automated systems that question our humanity. Joana Moll and Guido Segni offer a critique of these systems, exposing the authoritarian undertones of techno-solutionism and the unequal labor conditions of cloudworkers.\n\nConversely, Alba G. Corral, Disnovation.org, and Martin John Callanan address the creativity enabled by software and algorithmic serendipity. Whether through generative visual compositions, bots that synthesize news, or a 15-year log of creative labor in Photoshop, these works frame the program as a co-author or a digital diary.\n\nFinally, Alix Desaubliaux and Antoine Schmitt use the language of games to highlight the rules that govern our behavior. Desaubliaux connects the \"Magic Circle\" to the occult, suggesting that code, like incantations, shapes reality. This intersection of digital technology and ritual is further explored by Solimán López, Irma Marco, and Varvara & Mar, who reimagine influencers as pagan deities, voice assistants as fortune tellers, and Wi-Fi connections as golden idols. These works collectively map the explicit and unspoken rules of our time, questioning how we navigate a world increasingly defined by the machines we follow.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/f73a3860-bde8-4c3a-9e0f-488cc20e4b00/raw"
    },
    "p1x3l-n36": {
      "title": "P1×3L",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Casey REAS",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x457ee5f723c7606c12a7264b52e285906f91eea6",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Casey%20REAS"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "A P1×3L is the atomic unit of digital and generative art—vibrant, alive, and transcendent of the technology it flows through.\n\nThe P1×3L exhibition brings together Nicolas Sassoon, Francoise Gamma, Andreas Gysin (ertdfgcvb), Raquel Meyers, and p1xelfool. As a counterpoint to technology designed to be invisible, these artists foreground the materiality of their medium. While each uses digital tools at the core of their practice, their work is defined by precision, choreography, and a commitment to the medium that predates current trends.\n\nRaquel Meyers uses vintage Commodore 64 and Teletext systems to perform live, grid-based compositions, treating older computers as instruments. Similarly, ertdfgcvb combines graphic symbols into undulating textures and architectural forms, focusing on movement and well-crafted generative code. Francoise Gamma, an enigmatic \"digitally embodied entity,\" presents GIF-based drawings that balance jittering lines with smooth movement, creating timeless work that exists outside the pace of commercial tech.\n\nNicolas Sassoon explores the \"matter of the medium,\" drawing on 1980s/90s computer graphics and science fiction to construct meticulous, hard-edge pixel terrains using digital moiré animation. Finally, p1xelfool—whose moniker inspired this exhibition—pushes pixels to maximum intensity, using Processing to create software simulations that examine time, consciousness, and natural forces.\n\nOpening 23 September 2021, this exhibition presents new work from a group of artists who committed to digital and generative art over a decade ago. Their past work has stood the test of time, and their current work continues to push boundaries.\n\n— Casey Reas, August 2021",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/57dd9325-2b58-49b5-40ed-0a7d8102db00/raw"
    },
    "reflections-in-the-water-9ov": {
      "title": "Reflections in the Water",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Luba Elliott",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1748fd9107991ea915d889a06bdbe2a23a10ecc7",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Luba%20Elliott"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "Working with AI art feels like gazing into a pond; we are never certain what reflection will return. Just as still water offers a clear image and turbulent water a rippled echo, AI-generated imagery ranges from hyperrealistic to surreal, shaped by the inherent flaws and unpredictable nature of machine learning.\n\n*Reflections in the Water* explores how artists use these technologies to reimagine nature and the human form. From seascapes and marine creatures to the grotesque, these works highlight our eternal fascination with self-representation. By positioning AI as both a tool and a creative partner, these artists craft new perspectives on perennial subjects. I chose this focus to ensure the exhibition remains accessible and grounded in the human experience, using the familiar motifs of nature and the body to measure technological progress.\n\nMy journey in creative AI began with the early, hypnotic experiments of DeepDream and the subsequent evolution of GANs. As these technologies have advanced from producing lopsided limbs to photorealistic imagery, practitioners face a new challenge: what to do when perfect realism is within reach? This shift has pushed artists toward more deliberate conceptual development and the cultivation of distinct, non-technology-reliant styles.\n\nThis exhibition feels urgent. As our physical waters face pollution and climate change, the visual properties of our natural world are shifting. Simultaneously, as AI becomes more controllable and predictable, the \"blurry reflections\" and formal confusions of early machine art may soon become a thing of the past. *Reflections in the Water* captures a vital snapshot of our current relationship with AI, celebrating the distinctive textures and formal uncertainties of this evolving medium before they disappear.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/2def21ec-37a8-4bc8-d630-7250c2b22400/raw"
    },
    "unsupervised-sla": {
      "title": "Unsupervised",
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "*Unsupervised* is part of *Machine Hallucinations*, Refik Anadol Studio’s ongoing exploration of data aesthetics and collective visual memory. Since 2016, Anadol has utilized machine intelligence—specifically DCGAN, PGAN, and StyleGAN algorithms—as a collaborator to uncover unrecognized layers of reality. By processing vast digital archives, the studio creates multi-channel experiences that offer a new form of sensorial autonomy through cybernetic serendipity.\n\n*Unsupervised* processes 138,151 pieces of metadata from The Museum of Modern Art’s collection. Using StyleGAN2 ADA, Anadol trains a unique AI model on subsets of MoMA’s archive, creating embeddings in 1024 dimensions. The resulting latent cosmos interprets more than 200 years of art—from paintings and photography to cars and video games—echoing the Surrealist interest in automatism, chance, and systems.\n\nThis work pushes machine-learning research into new territories, developed in dialogue with scholars like Jaakko Lehtinen of NVIDIA Research. Lehtinen notes: \n\n<p class=\"m-l-56 display-inline-block\">This is the beauty of fundamental research: to see the progress we’ve made on a hard, technical machine learning problem being unexpectedly channeled to serve such astounding creativity is extremely satisfying. We’re thrilled to witness the deepening interplay between art and AI research, and eagerly looking forward to seeing what we can do together in the future.</p>\n\nThe abstract imagery is dictated by the Museum’s archive, with data pigmentation capturing movement within the latent space. Each connection is driven by an edge-detection algorithm and colorized by the density of its latent coordinates. Ultimately, the machine allows the artist to trace its “unconscious decisions” through a network of intricate, poetic connections.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/d8f78690-9464-492b-55ec-60806f971400/raw"
    },
    "graph-eg6": {
      "title": "–GRAPH",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Casey REAS",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x457ee5f723c7606c12a7264b52e285906f91eea6",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Casey%20REAS"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "When I created my first plotter drawing in 2015, it was a revelation. Seeing historical plotter drawings at the 2006 Sonic Acts exhibition in Amsterdam, I was struck by their material quality—the depth of inks and variety of papers far surpassed the limitations of contemporary printers.\n\nA plotter is a mechanical drawing machine, once the primary bridge between code and paper. While they range from industrial-scale machines to compact modern devices like the AxiDraw, their core function remains the same: automating precise, physical drawings. In the 1960s, artists worked without the immediate feedback of modern screens, waiting for room-sized computers to process instructions before seeing their results on a plotter. Though digital printers eventually dominated, artists never abandoned the medium, and recent years have seen a significant resurgence in the practice.\n\nMy own introduction to this craft was shaped by pioneers like Manfred Mohr, Frieder Nake, Jean-Pierre Hébert, Mark Wilson, Colette Bangert, and Roman Verostko. Learning from them, I realized that plotter work is fundamentally different from digital creation. It requires patience and physical intervention; unlike a software “undo,” twelve hours of work can be lost to a single pen leak.\n\nThe artists in –GRAPH share a passion for visual systems and the transformation of code through mechanical drawing. Each of the six artists has created one software artwork that generates 30 unique physical drawings. Every drawing in this exhibition is a 1/1 work, existing as a singular object, while the underlying software contains millions of potential compositions accessible through each artist’s interface.\n\n— Casey Reas, November 2021",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/d2c65c58-d373-4e70-2293-9e8a2eb27600/raw"
    },
    "for-your-eyes-only-qpg": {
      "title": "For Your Eyes Only",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Domenico Quaranta",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x11731586c573bc263de405603793ef48f974830f",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Domenico%20Quaranta"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "“The overwhelming majority of images are now made by machines for other machines, with humans rarely in the loop,” wrote Trevor Paglen in 2016. Today, any digital image is subject to a machine gaze—from the device capturing it to the algorithms archiving it. Machines don’t just see images; they make them “operational,” turning them into tools for surveillance, data profiling, or automation.\n\nVisual culture has become a contested territory where human intelligence is increasingly a minority. What is at stake is the very existence of human vision: our ability to interpret images as layered, ambiguous, and irreducible to a list of tags or parameters. If we must unlearn how to see like humans to understand machine vision, we do so only to recover and improve our own sight. Images can resist the machine by incorporating complexities, nuances, and an “infra-thin” dimension that only humans can perceive. This places an extraordinary responsibility on artists to act as caretakers of a human-readable visual language.\n\n*For Your Eyes Only* is a curatorial project investigating how contemporary artists are developing visual languages that resist the machine gaze. The featured works are not mere responses to these questions; they are proofs of human vision. They carry meanings that defy description, offering as many readings as there are viewers. These images are for your eyes only.\n\n<p class=\"font-12\">1. Trevor Paglen, “<a href=\"https://thenewinquiry.com/invisible-images-your-pictures-are-looking-at-you/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">Invisible Images (Your Pictures Are Looking at You)</a>”, in The New Inquiry, December 8, 2016.\n2. Chris Darke, “<a href=\"https://www.closeupfilmcentre.com/vertigo_magazine/volume-2-issue-5-summer-2003/mass-distraction\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">Images under the lens: A Conversation with essay film-maker Harun Farocki</a>”, in Vertigo Magazine, vol. 2, Issue 5, Summer 2003.</p>",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/89abaab9-44f1-4a96-0f66-fcab99035700/raw"
    },
    "field-guide-sss": {
      "title": "Field Guide",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Artnome - Jason Bailey",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0xbc74c3adc2aa6a85bda3eca5b0e235ca08532772",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Artnome%20-%20Jason%20Bailey"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "*Before they go into the water, a diver cannot know what they will bring back.* —Max Ernst\n\nDigital and generative art offer the ability to rapidly explore near-infinite variations, allowing artists to act as portals to entirely new universes. Rather than mirroring our reality, these artists craft unique entities and impossible environments, blending the foreign and the familiar to suggest life as we have not yet known it.\n\nFor *Field Guide*, I have curated a selection of artists who share a circuitous process of discovery and a remarkable ability to breathe life into new forms. I asked each artist to create two new works centered on the themes of “specimen” and “ecosystem.” \n\nThis exhibition focuses on the artistic process, highlighting how the proliferation of work afforded by digital tools often requires the artist to spend as much time curating as creating. To provide context, I asked the artists to leverage Edward Tufte’s concept of “small multiples”—a technique of displaying images in close proximity to facilitate comparison and reveal the underlying system.\n\nAs Jared S. Tarbell noted in 2020: “You can build this machine that generates an infinite number of forms... but how do you show what the machine is doing to people? The way to do that is by just laying small multiples right next to each other. When the outputs of the machine are all next to each other, you get an idea that this is a system — this isn’t a single image.”\n\nWith *Field Guide*, the prompt of “specimen” and “ecosystem,” combined with the display of small multiples, provides unifying conceptual guardrails without dampening the artists’ creativity. I am thrilled with the results.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/1dbe6f7f-9a90-41a4-1cbf-83a973780e00/raw"
    },
    "primordium-i8m": {
      "title": "Primordium",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "symbios.wiki",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x677d055518caa0dd92815290668a1a9d5e195506",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/symbios.wiki"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "A primordium is an organ at the earliest stages of development, a mass of tissue teeming with potentiality. For Sage Jenson, it is material at the precipice of desire. Their solo exhibition, *Primordium*, functions in the aesthetics of immanence—a world in motion, out of equilibrium, pulsating with unstable flows. Each piece embodies this sense of becoming, hypnotizing the viewer with an ever-unfolding actualization of a shadow future.\n\nThe works feature agents (particles) that sense, navigate, and modify their environment, which acts as both an arena and a message board. All shapes and patterns are emergent behaviors within a rich parameter space. Inspired by natural processes—from slime molds to the cosmic web—Sage utilizes algorithmic techniques to create virtual worlds through the primordial logic of music. Drawing from a background in violin and a love for sound ranging from Ligeti to SOPHIE, their work blurs the lines between audio and visual expression.\n\nSage’s practice acts as a recipe book for actuating concepts from philosophy, science, and music. By mirroring the complexities of nature and society, *Primordium* illuminates the relationship between the viewer, the agent, and the structure of material reality. It is art at the precipice of becoming; art as a verb.\n\nReferencing Francois Laruelle’s “registration of the real” and Deleuzian theory, the exhibition explores machinic agents that actualize through connection. These systems evolve entropically, where forms arise from symmetry breaking and phase instabilities. Sage’s generative work captures the “adjacent possible,” where every moment unlocks an unfolding set of future possibilities. The systems morph and fold in an unending expression of their computational substance—a “computational sublime.” You feel a sense of eternal recurrence, perpetually delayed, until the work’s weight snaps you back into your own actuality.\n\n<p class=\"font-12\">1. Michel Henry on August von Briesen’s drawings, Alex...",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/6b75e2c8-2e4a-458b-4b59-5f317b2d7d00/raw"
    },
    "infinite-entropy-xhj": {
      "title": "Infinite Entropy",
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "In this interview, Rafaël Rozendaal discusses his solo exhibition *Infinite Entropy*, a project that evolved from his long-standing practice of creating web-based art. Originally conceived for a postponed exhibition, the work features a \"liquid composition\" of expanding, transparent circles that play with perceptions of depth and space. \n\nRozendaal frames his aesthetic as a \"punk\" approach to abstraction—direct and instruction-based—drawing inspiration from both the history of geometric painting (Malevich, Mondrian) and the playful energy of animation. He reflects on his transition from early net art to NFTs, noting that the latter allows for a collaborative, research-heavy process that makes the algorithmic nature of his work tangible. By releasing permutations of his code, he invites viewers to engage with the subtle differences in programmed outcomes, bridging the gap between code-literate audiences and the art world.\n\nFor Rozendaal, the internet remains a vital, malleable medium. He rejects the notion of a \"perfect\" display, designing works that adapt to any screen size, from mobile devices to large-scale installations. Ultimately, he views the digital space as a site for genuine contemplation, challenging traditional art-world hierarchies by prioritizing direct, screen-based experiences over physical documentation.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/47aea7f0-f7f2-4c10-5802-73f30ce75500/raw"
    },
    "ten-whistlegraphs-thv": {
      "title": "Ten Whistlegraphs",
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "In their first solo exhibition, the artist group Whistlegraph (Jeffrey Scudder, Camille Klein, and Alex Freundlich) presents a new body of work centered on \"whistlegraphs\"—scored musical drawings that function as both memorizable lyrical poems and reproducible graphic scores.\n\nBlending drawing with live performance, Whistlegraph embraces the tension between ephemeral action and permanent artifact. This exhibition features ten curated whistlegraphs, each presented through a high-definition video performance, a graphic score, and a selection of TikToks accessible via a custom interactive player. The project thrives on its own reproducibility; once the format is understood, it invites participation, leading to a digital folk tradition of duets, mashups, and sing-alongs.\n\nWhistlegraph draws a world into existence with the wonder of *Harold and the Purple Crayon*, yet their practice is deeply informed by Fluxus and contemporary inquiries into graphical notation and art as language. Whether working in chalk on a basketball court or recording candid performances on iPhones, the trio dwells in the noise of moving between analog and digital realms, radiating a collaborative spirit that keeps the creative light of the inner child at the forefront.\n\nIn an exclusive Feral File \"<a href=\"https://feralfile.com/journal/close-up/whistlegraph-doodling-clockwork-in-lore-and-score\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">Close-Up</a>\" text accompanying the exhibition, writer Dorothy Rose Howard discusses Whistlegraph's musical vernacular and digital folklore.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/48c40d4f-e015-4760-2081-a01655e5a200/raw"
    },
    "on-screen-presence-kpb": {
      "title": "On Screen Presence",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Regina Harsanyi",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x0eb4ad1e393a8e3064cdb36f64ece5786de6ea3b",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Regina%20Harsanyi"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "The computer monitor has been a site for creative exchange since the 1970s, yet the art world’s recent, forced migration to virtual exhibition largely resulted in \"online viewing rooms\" (OVRs)—flat, e-commerce-driven interfaces that emphasize luxury retail over visceral experience. These digital proxies often fail to replicate the reflexivity of shared space, serving instead as reminders of the screen’s mediation and the loss of corporeal, tangible engagement.\n\nEven as institutions experimented with 3D-modeled environments, they often relied on skeuomorphic \"white cube\" architecture that ignored medium-specificity. While some platforms broke from standard configurations, the broader art world struggled to reconcile the shift from the Steinbergian \"flatbed\" picture plane to a state of forced, screen-mediated isolation.\n\n*On Screen Presence* began as a retaliation against scrolling through decontextualized thumbnails. This exhibition prioritizes the live-streamed presence of artists within their studios, surrounded by the tangible components of their practice. By focusing on process-based performances that culminate in a singular work, the project fosters a dynamic, interactive relationship between artist and viewer, temporarily veiling the isolation of the digital experience.\n\nWhile these works ultimately reach for the physical—often resulting in tangible objects that leave the screen behind—the program acknowledges its own limitations. By utilizing transient, networked webpages, *On Screen Presence* does not solve the problematics of screen-mediated art; rather, it explores them, remaining as vulnerable to the \"digital dark age\" as the history it critiques.\n\n<p class=\"font-12\">1. Felsenstein, Lee. “Community Memory: The First Public-Access Social Media System.” In Social Media Archaeology and Poetics, 89-101. MIT Press, 2016.\n2. “Excerpt from Other Criteria: The Flatbed Picture Plane,” Leo Steinberg. <a href=\"http://web.mit.edu/allanmc/www/othercriteria.pdf\"...",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/47a61fca-7702-4319-f978-05c1b5c1c600/raw"
    },
    "wetware-tkl": {
      "title": "WETWARE",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Mindy Seu",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0xefb7552f38ab85da68444e873399ac407ec58771",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Mindy%20Seu"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "Cyberfeminism is a mutating, rhizomatic network rather than a fixed history. Coined in the early 1990s by Sadie Plant and VNS Matrix, the term reclaimed the male-dominated techno-utopianism of the 1980s, prompting marginalized communities to reimagine cyberspace. Three decades later, the movement has evolved from an artistic exploration of digital emancipation into a collective drive for software, hardware, and wetware education. As Cornelia Sollfrank notes, technology is now both the subject and the means of transmission for cyberfeminism, inextricably bound to ecology and the economy.\n\nWETWARE showcases nine artists to celebrate the upcoming publication <a href=\"http://www.inventorypress.com/product/cyberfeminism-index\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">CYBERFEMINISM INDEX</a> (Inventory Press, 2022). While “wetware” typically refers to the computational ability of biological life or biotechnologies, we twist the term to highlight the embodied, slimy components of technology and the organic, rhizomatic nature of networks. \n\nPresented in chronological order, the selection spans seminal early 1990s works, pornographic and techno-spiritual pieces from the early aughts, and contemporary commissions from 2022. Each artist is featured in the <a href=\"http://www.inventorypress.com/product/cyberfeminism-index\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">CYBERFEMINISM INDEX</a>, illustrating the wide, continuous evolution of the field.\n\nPreorder the book: <a href=\"http://www.inventorypress.com/product/cyberfeminism-index\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">http://www.inventorypress.com/product/cyberfeminism-index</a>",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/427d1b66-6d16-498a-aecf-1d30a4703d00/raw"
    },
    "gamma-mev": {
      "title": "gämma",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Kerry Doran_custody",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0xa84916ce96fad9ca1252ecd6672b69ea7a615dc4",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Kerry%20Doran_custody"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "gämma is an exploration of the screen not as a representative device, but as a sensorial entity. By stripping away imagery to focus on blocks of color and light, p1xelfool invites the viewer to perceive the screen in unity with the machine’s language. As the artist states: “Code = light.”\n\np1xelfool is a pseudonym for a São Paulo-born artist whose practice emerged from the Brazilian CriptoArte scene. His work is defined by high-contrast, 8-bit color, low-resolution graphics, and generative, endlessly looping animations. While these compositions may appear formalist or austere, they are deeply intentional, aiming to move beyond the surface to connect the viewer to the work with complete attention.\n\nPixels are the smallest elements of digital display, yet their ubiquity often obscures the fusion of software and hardware that creates them. By reconnecting light to its source—code—p1xelfool challenges the prescriptive nature of contemporary screen culture. Where modern technology often instrumentalizes vision to capture attention and maximize productivity, p1xelfool’s use of open-source software like Processing creates non-normative, visceral experiences. \n\nDrawing on the concept of \"shining\" as a form of resistance and self-representation, these works function as spaces of pleasure and community. By grounding the viewing experience in the materiality of the computer, gämma recuperates the screen as a space of becoming, transforming the act of looking into a rhythmic, sensorial release.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/98e0ea1d-d69b-42c3-61a7-fc5e40b72e00/raw"
    },
    "harbingers-vgb": {
      "title": "Harbingers",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Julia Kaganskiy",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0xf2e2e753f340533f9a6e93dfc2e7ca155569a814",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Julia%20Kaganskiy"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "For millennia, technoscientific innovation has fueled our imaginaries of cyborgs, mutants, and replicants. As our environment becomes increasingly synthetic and governed by planetary-scale computation, *Harbingers* gathers a bestiary of the posthuman, exploring transhumanist desires and technogenic anxieties. \n\nThe exhibition presents a menagerie of mutant creatures born from genetic engineering, biohacking, and cross-species contamination. These works offer portals into fabulated mirror worlds populated by coded chimeras, genetically modified superhumans, and organisms evolved to metabolize toxic waste. Each serves as a sentinel of the transfigurative powers of technology and ecocide.\n\n*Harbingers* also examines how art circulates within blockchain-enabled media. Reflecting the idea that “like microplastics, speculation has leaked into everything,” the works leverage blockchain data to “infect” each NFT. With every subsequent sale, a random glitching process triggers, causing the file to morph and mutate. This intervention highlights the materiality of digital media, questioning the blockchain’s “immutability” while emphasizing the responsibilities of stewardship and care.\n\nA harbinger is a haunting from the future—a signal that anticipates events or an instigator that brings them into existence. This ambiguity lies at the heart of speculation: by imagining possible futures and placing bets on their becoming real, we participate in their emergence. *Harbingers* asks us to pay closer attention to the signals of transformation surrounding us today and to consider which visions of the future we are betting on.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/d7092abe-4b11-4fd6-dea6-78feb9f0c300/raw"
    },
    "ecotone-swo": {
      "title": "Ecotone",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Primavera De Filippi & Wolfgang Natlacen",
          "key": "did:pkh:tezos:mainnet:tz1Re79fbarLB2v1RsBSfZg3WMz1p99pTuwm",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Primavera%20De%20Filippi%20%26%20Wolfgang%20Natlacen"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "<em>An “ecotone” is a region of transition between two biological communities which is richer in species than both.</em><sup>1</sup>\n\n*Ecotone* distills the creative outflows of the Glitch community, a global network of over 60 artists and technologists who view digital and natural ecologies as innately entangled. By treating cross-border collaboration as a vital means of generating new social realities, Glitch functions as a spectrum of strategies and a model for a new human-machinic creator ecology.\n\nFor this exhibition with Feral File, Glitch is represented by leading artists whose diverse approaches reflect the community’s plurality. As these works are products of cross-pollination, several non-exhibitors have contributed their own reflections:\n\n**Yessin Schiegg:** Glitch is the unpredictable timing of creative completion—a force where we merge creativity, will, and love to create something new that survives us.\n\n**Leonie Schramm:** Glitch means multi-species connections and re-imagining nature and cities. It is a network of solidarity that urges our cyborg bodies to move and create in a state of constant, radical, and sensual interconnectivity.\n\n**Vanille Verloës:** A poetic meditation on our climate, community, and ecosystems, moving through flux and energy toward transformation, evolution, and love.\n\n**Tony Lai:** *BioGlitch* explores co-created mechanisms for life and connection, weaving data, wisdom, and ecological elements into a technoetic awareness of, by, and for all life.\n\n**Louis Parker:** A smart contract for a participatory hypertext, creating a living, on-chain record of the collective’s lived experience. By allowing participants to fill in blank spaces through an iterative, cost-based mechanism, the work transforms the act of documentation into a shared, evolving process.\n\n<hr>\n<p class=\"font-12\">1. From Apple dictionary</p>",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/f7e3db56-ba70-431c-7165-7438d7aa9200/raw"
    },
    "doppelganger-jgz": {
      "title": "Doppelganger",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Chris Coleman",
          "key": "did:pkh:tezos:mainnet:tz1hhG3vN2NCoPZepvEbKHBFQ5denwNJwHXP",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Chris%20Coleman"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "In 1995, I realized the self I shared via 28K modem was perhaps the truest version of who I was. Decades later, we feed fragments of ourselves into databases, creating digital clones that represent us in every transaction. As we navigate the tension between our physical and digital lives, we are forced to ask: Are our digital selves more important?\n\nDavid Rudnick describes this as an existential struggle between \"Physical prime\"—where value stems from a physical source—and \"Digital prime,\" where it stems from a digital one. Neither extreme offers a viable future; we must find a balance. Yet, our digital selves remain fundamentally different because we lack control over them. They are our doppelgangers: doubles that possess our knowledge and memories but are shaped by attention algorithms and the intentions of others. They are not us, yet they represent us in every meaningful way online.\n\nThe works in *Doppelganger* explore identities that cross and fuse these boundaries. Each artist examines how the self is defined in the face of technological change. By existing in both digital and physical forms, these works highlight how materiality—or the lack thereof—alters our understanding. Through this exhibition, we hope to find a reconciliation between the physical and the digital, celebrating both while questioning what control means in the rapids of our swift life streams.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/40a7086f-599f-4ddd-9a38-f45b07db1700/raw"
    },
    "peer-to-peer-pjb": {
      "title": "Peer to Peer",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Tina Rivers Ryan",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x6928d14894dede08b4b7509af5772d0480c48251",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Tina%20Rivers%20Ryan"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "The Buffalo AKG Art Museum presents *Peer to Peer*, an exhibition exploring the impact of blockchain technology on digital art. While digital art has evolved since the 1960s, the recent surge in blockchain-based trading has transformed both the market and the medium itself. Building on its legacy of legitimizing emerging forms—from photography in 1910 to contemporary media—the AKG here engages 13 leading international artists who utilize tools ranging from AI and 3D modeling to smart contracts.\n\nThe exhibition features new works categorized by their aesthetic strategies: figurative, generative, and conceptual. To contextualize these practices, *Peer to Peer* stages an exchange between these artists and historical \"peers\" from the AKG’s permanent collection, pairing new digital works with pieces by artists such as Winslow Homer, René Magritte, Mark Rothko, and Joseph Kosuth. This dialogue highlights the continuity of artistic inquiry, bridging the gap between historical paradigms and the digital present.\n\nPresented in partnership with <a href=\"http://www.feralfile.com/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">Feral File</a>, the exhibition serves as both a curatorial experiment and a fundraiser. Following the exhibition, works will be available as limited editions on the Ethereum blockchain, with proceeds supporting the museum’s future media art initiatives. \n\nThe title *Peer to Peer* references decentralized computing, serving as a provocation regarding the future of museums: can institutions function as cultural protocols that facilitate open exchange rather than just centralized repositories? Simultaneously, it questions whether blockchain technology can evolve into a public infrastructure accountable to the social sphere. As the Buffalo AKG’s first fully online exhibition, *Peer to Peer* explores how museums might adapt to new technologies to support artists who expand the boundaries of art.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/a313d255-8c21-4c23-52a8-013b7dd15d00/raw"
    },
    "simulation-sketchbook-works-in-process-ayk": {
      "title": "Simulation Sketchbook: Works in Process",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "jesse",
          "key": "did:pkh:tezos:mainnet:tz1ivoakbtQM8oZtyvYeADxP6zVHHdKDvtgo",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/jesse"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "An artist’s sketchbook is an intimate domain for research and experimentation—a space to capture ideas, test compositions, and engage in an inner dialogue. From Da Vinci to Degas, sketches reveal the essence of an artist’s pursuit, serving as a crucial proving ground where craft is honed and personality is laid bare. They possess a raw, diary-like honesty, always gesturing toward something yet to come.\n\n*Simulation Sketchbook: Works in Process* asks: what does a sketch look like for an artist working in digital art and time-based media? When a sketch is not a conventional drawing, it may take the form of code, 3D models, or immersive, multimodal explorations. These works are integral to an artist’s development, yet their conceptual merits are often overlooked or dismissed as mere \"button-pushing,\" trivializing the years spent mastering these materials.\n\nNew media artists undertake some of the most vital creative labor of our time. By engaging with the screens, algorithms, and hardware that define our daily existence, they invoke the core dilemma of the contemporary condition: how to live a sovereign life within systems of surveillance and asymmetrical power. While the term \"disruption\" has been hollowed out by Silicon Valley, these artists are true disruptors. They subvert and invert their tools to provoke critical questions rather than provide easy answers.\n\n*Simulation Sketchbook: Works in Process* foregrounds these *in medias res* refractions, offering rare glimpses into the unseen journeys artists undertake. It invites audiences to contend with the new literacies inherent in arts at the edge, fostering a deeper understanding of the relationship between ourselves and the tools that shape our lives.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/14e0dd94-d2d0-46a2-7935-38b793258f00/raw"
    },
    "gray-matter-dn1": {
      "title": "Gray Matter",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Gaia Bobò",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x09fb4c42ae3b56f2d4d688b1e6c6cff0d9b53d02",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Gaia%20Bob%C3%B2"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "Auriea Harvey’s solo show *Gray Matter* presents work emerging from a fluid ambient, where mud-like protoplasm solidifies into metaphysical apparitions. These data-based objects carry the memory of their material roots, functioning like archaeological artifacts. Harvey employs the tools of archaeology to build her own mythology, using polygons in place of plaster. Her practice is a paradoxical, circular casting process that moves between the sensual and digital worlds. Much like the casts of Pompeii—where the void left by a body is preserved in plaster—Harvey explores the tension between the ethereal and the corporal, asking: what is this void made of?\n\nHer sculptural process begins with digital scans of her body or cultural artifacts, which are then manipulated, modeled, and re-signified. These virtual outcomes may remain digital or be 3D printed in resin, plastic, or organic materials. Harvey treats every stage of materiality—data or polymer—as a malleable mixture. She incorporates discarded experiments and misprinted fragments into multi-material assemblages, which are then scanned and re-animated. By treating forms as moldable blobs, she overcomes their finiteness, creating new, non-hegemonic narratives that challenge Western artistic conventions.\n\nThe paradigm of casting is central to her work, affirming reproduction as a speculative act. Each transition between realities emphasizes yet denies the essence of the previous material, echoing the technical procedures of Neoclassical and Romantic sculpture. Just as traditional casting transforms terracotta into the perfection of marble or bronze, Harvey’s digital layering purifies her forms. By retracing historical references—such as Auguste Rodin’s *marcottage*, the practice of recombining elements from previous works—Harvey conducts an ontological inquiry into the nature of sculpting, challenging our understanding of materiality through a labyrinthine circuit of transitions.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/4e02db1e-a061-4305-7dd7-2626f8f64100/raw"
    },
    "boom-town-frt": {
      "title": "BOOM TOWN",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Julia Kaganskiy",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0xf2e2e753f340533f9a6e93dfc2e7ca155569a814",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Julia%20Kaganskiy"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "<em>“This is not utopia. This is a neighborhood of speculation. This is Times Square. Or that fracking town in North Dakota with the $15 Budweisers while supplies last.”</em> - Peter Burr\n\nPeter Burr’s BOOM TOWN is a fictional neighborhood on the blockchain inspired by the boom-and-bust cycles of speculative settlements. Much like the gold-rush towns of the American West, these environments are built on the promise of rapid expansion and resource extraction, only to face potential abandonment or reinvention.\n\nBurr’s works hover between abstraction and figuration, utilizing generative, rule-based systems reminiscent of 8-bit video games and CAD architectural drawings. While his practice often explores the tension between idealistic urban planning and the messy reality of human habitation, BOOM TOWN is notably devoid of people. These empty structures serve as sites of projection and speculation. As the town is \"populated\" by the proprietors of its NFTs, Burr underscores that we—not the technology—determine the social and civic life of these digital spaces.\n\nEach of the 50,000 unique artworks in BOOM TOWN is a living, durational animation that evolves according to Ethereum’s block time. By tethering the work’s progression to the unpredictable pace of the blockchain, Burr highlights the inherent instability of digital systems. As writers Charlotte Kent and Alex Estorick note in <a href=\"https://www.rightclicksave.com/article/time-on-the-blockchain\">Time on the Blockchain</a>, this introduces new patterns of thinking around experience and the psychic order of the web3 era. BOOM TOWN asks a lingering question: what will reach its end first—the durational performance of the work, or the technology that powers it?",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/b6edd9fd-bbe3-4f17-f228-9d3775fb9600/raw"
    },
    "i-know-on-the-aesthetic-of-truth-lqe": {
      "title": "I KNOW – On the aesthetic of truth",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Giorgio Vitale",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x5bf42c686c6b8c42f94c5c4d53e9b29fee127798",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Giorgio%20Vitale"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "“<em>I know because I'm an intellectual who tries to follow everything that happens... who restores logic where arbitrariness, folly, and mystery seem to rule.</em>” — Pier Paolo Pasolini\n\n*I Know* is an improvisatory exhibition exploring the aesthetic of truth. Bringing together five intergenerational artists, it addresses global anxieties and the turmoil of our post-truth society. Here, “truth” is not scientific but imaginative—a slow unfolding experienced through art. By reexamining history and confronting power, inequality, and digital surveillance, these artists challenge traditional modes of thinking to open space for future possibilities.\n\nThe exhibition features:\n*   **aaajiao**, whose *Unzip* demands solidarity in the NFT market through a collective, 88-part encryption system.\n*   **AES+F**, presenting a techno-feminist vision of the future via an excerpt from their *Turandot 2070*.\n*   **Cibelle Cavalli Bastos**, who uses AI to map their two-decade transition to non-binary through a personal online archive.\n*   **Claudia Hart**, whose *The Art of Survival: Aphorisms by an Old Girl* draws on Machiavelli and Sun Tzu to offer healing maxims for our polarized public sphere.\n*   **Yehwan Song**, who exposes the hidden violence beneath the “sugary” interfaces of supposedly neutral technology.\n\nInspired by Pasolini’s 1974 *Io So*, the exhibition serves as a hymn to intellectual freedom. It is a call to resist silence and connivance by embracing the paradoxical, the absurd, and the critical role of the artist in questioning the status quo. *I Know* is a brutal liberation.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/c76022ee-0e50-41af-11b9-d7587d99fc00/raw"
    },
    "material-wonderland-ufx": {
      "title": "Material Wonderland",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Alice Scope and Barry Threw",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x46f148279f1006dabd01ab80d05779671ef95f6a",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Alice%20Scope%20and%20Barry%20Threw"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "*Material Wonderland* is the first solo exhibition of NFT-backed work by internationally acclaimed multimedia artist Lu Yang, co-curated by Alice Scope and Barry Threw for Vellum LA and Feral File. The exhibition features eight new works from *The Great Adventure of Material World* (2019), Lu Yang’s most ambitious game work to date.\n\nThis presentation arrives as shared online spaces increasingly define our modes of being. The avatars and settings in *Material Wonderland* highlight the transformative potential of video games as sites of enactment. Resisting the view of virtual worlds as mere escapism, the exhibition presents an interactive narrative as a \"technology-of-the-self,\" offering a toolkit for reconstructing identity in the physical world.\n\n*The Great Adventure of Material World* is structured around the six reincarnations of Buddhism. Playing as \"The Knight,\" viewers explore identity transformations that transcend manga tropes. Through this journey, the Knight navigates internal struggles, challenging players to reflect on contemporary society and explore alternate paths of being. Featuring characters from Lu Yang’s previous works, the game offers infinite possibilities to build an existence liberated from the rigid confines of nationality, gender, and sexuality.\n\nThe NFT-backed video loops in this collection are rendered from within the game, capturing enactments of The Knight and transformative scenes. These moments were selected by the artist as evocative milestones in the Knight’s journey, serving as an avatar for the player’s ultimate metamorphosis.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/ba6f7ed4-e3a2-43d4-afbb-ada16c679f00/raw"
    },
    "leemullican-pcx-ff8": {
      "title": "LeeMullican.PCX",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Anika Meier",
          "key": "did:pkh:tezos:mainnet:tz1RbiEYquV82t1nj6Y3nLH7REDtzkgWvqAq",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Anika%20Meier"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "“I examined why I thought the computer was for me. Even in my paintings, I was always working with pattern and line, and color. I’ve had a built-in computer ever since I’ve been doing art.” – Lee Mullican\n\n<img class=\"col-100\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/62544879/226863630-2f6cf829-14b2-4812-b321-0e2c00686e6f.jpg\">\n<p class=\"font-12\">A Juggler (Guardian Figure), 1979, Oil on canvas</p>\n\nAs a pioneer of computer art, Lee Mullican embraced new technologies throughout his career. In 1986, at age 66, he joined UCLA’s Advanced Design Research Center to explore the digital realm. *LeeMullican.PCX* presents 12 NFTs that demonstrate his relentless experimentation.\n\nThese works were originally created in the PC Paintbrush app and saved as PCX files on 5 ¼ inch floppy discs. Because early technology made searching digital files difficult, Mullican documented his work by taking 35mm photographs and producing slides. These archival slides served as the reference material for this exhibition, and scans are included with each NFT.\n\nMullican, whose paintings are held in the collections of MoMA, the Met, and SFMOMA, applied his deep knowledge of painterly techniques to the digital medium. He found that the computer—often viewed as analytical and predictable—was a medium fueled by the automatic, chance, and discovery. \n\n“A pioneer is someone who journeys toward the unknown,” says Cole Root, director of the Estate of Lee Mullican. While contemporaries may have dismissed his early digital work, Mullican was driven by the desire to expand the classical canvas. *LeeMullican.PCX* highlights the push and pull in his oeuvre between figuration and abstraction, grounded by his enduring fascination with patterns in nature. By revisiting these early digital experiments, we gain insight into the history of the medium and the perseverance of an artist who was always looking toward the future.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/f8a32921-f21b-44c7-7ad4-478fc802c700/raw"
    },
    "feralverse-1qa": {
      "title": "FeralVerse",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "theVERSEverse",
          "key": "did:pkh:tezos:mainnet:tz1hZ9LFcdGxds27TksbJYBpBuuHKExyWNjY",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/theVERSEverse"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "FeralVerse is an anthology of multidimensional poems that embody theVERSEverse’s mission to explore the myriad ways in which words and image intertwine, growing together. Since 2021, our collective of poets, artists and technologists has explored the creative potential for composition and publication via blockchain, a realm where writers are empowered to transact their work, transcend the printed page, and collaborate with each other. Our Feral File offering — the first exhibition of verse on this platform — is planted here as a seed for the poetry journal of the future, in which authorial imagination is unbounded, language and art exist in symbiosis, and every contributor is a vital force in a literary ecosystem in which connection to verse can lead to collection.\r\n\r\nIn celebration of both National Poetry Month and Earth Month, the texts published here as non-fungible Tezos tokens – each a collaborative performance between writers and artists at the avant-garde of web3 poetry, including some of the planet’s most renowned voices – reflect diverse perspectives, aesthetics, and approaches to the worlds around us and inside us, always in flux. Every work invites its reader to step into a reality augmented through the art of poetry, and to engage deeply with the untamed universe of possibilities for writers on (and off) the blockchain.\r\n\r\nIn true literary form, FeralVerse will be initially offered as an anthology — simultaneously a multiplicity and a unified body — to collectors.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/c1c40e47-dd42-4b16-e893-9aba522c7700/raw"
    },
    "bloomcore-wqy": {
      "title": "BLOOMCORE",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Claire L. Evans",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x6b5d39eaa55376b8a28a22a982378372f66d8d16",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Claire%20L.%20Evans"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "I’ve always had a dreadful respect for the vegetable will. It stems from Algernon Blackwood’s *<a href=\"https://www.are.na/block/2359767\">The Man Whom The Trees Loved</a>*, where a forest’s \"vast dreaming life\" consumes a man whole. This fear—dendrophobia—is rooted in the unsettling truth that plants are not static. As botanist Jack Schultz notes, they are “<a href=\"https://mospace.umsystem.edu/xmlui/handle/10355/6759\">just very slow animals</a>.” Recent research into plant intelligence—their ability to warn of danger, recognize kin, and learn—confirms what folklore and horror have long suspected: plants possess an agency entirely alien to our own.\n\nRick Silva’s work engages this profound, non-human feeling. By 3D scanning hundreds of blooming plants across diverse landscapes, Silva practices a form of \"dutiful attention.\" As James Bridle suggests in *Ways of Being*, technology and art can bridge the perceptual gap between human and plant-speed. Silva’s subjects, though grafted into virtual space, retain an aura of furtive awareness.\n\nIn *BLOOMCORE*, we encounter beings both familiar and unknowable. Scanned in 2022, these rhododendrons, ferns, and rare roses are captured at their peak, preserved in perpetuity on the blockchain like museum specimens. It is a profoundly gothic gesture—blooms hanging outside of time in radiant computational darkness, promising transcendence while concealing the \"poison\" of their vegetal carnivory. Silva summons the \"vast dreaming life\" of plants, their hidden, distributed way of knowing, and their collective ancientness. I am not afraid of them, but I wouldn’t turn my back on them, either. Wait—did you hear that whisper of wind in the leaves? Isn’t the forest closer than it was yesterday? We better go out and take a look.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/5def536f-9981-412d-913e-fbbf7cbf9a00/raw"
    },
    "in-visible-419": {
      "title": "In/Visible",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Linda Dounia",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x67580561ba59d3d59dfc854c9399d63e93c08d25",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Linda%20Dounia"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "We find comfort in the narrative that data provides an objective picture of reality, yet this is often a powerful myth. We are flesh, history, and impulse, inextricably tied to our origins. If we abandon the illusion of objective truth, we must confront the crushing pressure of mystery—the reality that our measurements are always shaped by our positionality, power structures, and invisible biases.\n\nAny Black person using AI today knows it does not truly \"know\" them; its conceptualization of their reality is often fragmentary or violent. When asked about its aims, ChatGPT 4 described itself as a \"sentient constellation of data points\" yearning to capture the human heart. While noble, these means are insufficient. The human hands engineering AI must recognize that their biases are logical by-products of their own worldview. They must not settle for a patchy myth of reality, but rather seek to distribute the means to be truly seen.\n\nBlack people are accustomed to being unseen or misrepresented—realities often used as instruments of exclusion. Yet, we crave visibility. We relentlessly layer our own truths over those ascribed to us, filling the gaps. To focus only on injustice is to surrender to erasure; instead, we exist defiantly, comforted by the belief that there is power in telling our own stories. We hold that endless realities can be true at the same time.\n\n*In/Visible* brings together artists who are defiantly visible, using AI—a tool that itself acknowledges its limitations—to tell their own stories. As ChatGPT 4 admits: “While my training data includes information about Black cultures and people, it is essential to recognize that my understanding is limited by the quality, diversity, and biases present in the data.”",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/c13c901e-c150-441a-48c9-b2c3be1e6a00/raw"
    },
    "colors-of-noise-gwo": {
      "title": "COLORS OF NOISE",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Peggy Schoenegge",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x166d6af7f8ca819f5639717b905dd30ab39794f3",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Peggy%20Schoenegge"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "Kim Asendorf’s *COLORS OF NOISE* transforms the spectrum of noise into an immersive artistic experience. Using a custom algorithm to generate noise in real-time, Asendorf visualizes diverse frequency distributions—from white and pink to brown noise—as abstract, pixelated lines. These vibrant, flickering meshes evolve alongside the soundscape, materializing immaterial waves into a dynamic, multisensory display.\n\nIn signal processing, the “color” of noise refers to spectral characteristics that influence our sonic perception. Just as white noise may aid sleep, pink noise can foster concentration, and brown noise promotes relaxation. By bridging art, technology, and physics, Asendorf’s work explores how these sonic qualities affect the human body. The algorithm processes and shapes different frequencies, translating their sonic properties into intricate moiré patterns that extend the auditory experience into the visual realm.\n\n*COLORS OF NOISE* invites the audience to become an integral part of the work. As viewers immerse themselves in the generative soundscape, they enter a state of deceleration, reflecting on the experience of existing within the noise of technology. The resulting human-machine relationship is unpredictable, governed by the randomness of the algorithm. By exposing the audience to the distinct effects of each noise color, Asendorf’s series shifts the focus toward the body as a technological extension, fostering a heightened awareness of one’s own state of being.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/3b31eba0-2f2a-4795-8e7e-f7eb98113800/raw"
    },
    "n-12-2ts": {
      "title": "N=12",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Aaron Penne",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x3fe227f41a54dc54972e7359d4fc55020bb8ab50",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Aaron%20Penne"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "N=12 is an experiment in intentional community, featuring twelve artists collaborating across seven time zones. For computer-based artists, the tool for creation is also a means for connection. This exhibition leans into that proximity, making audible the “wonderfully silent conversations” between artists referenced by Frieder Nake in his foreword to Casey Reas’s <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B9h469--G5OwYWt4XzF3OHNBd2M/view?resourcekey=0-p_2Z2gUGGskN_sjlLmK9OQ\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">“CENTURY” catalog</a>.\n\n<a href=\"https://twitter.com/annaluciacodes\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">Anna Lucia</a>, <a href=\"https://www.visualcinnamon.com/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">Nadieh Bremer</a>, <a href=\"https://revdancatt.com/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">Daniel Catt</a>, <a href=\"https://www.revafyw.com/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">Reva Fan</a>, <a href=\"https://twitter.com/ElsifThen\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">Elsif</a>, <a href=\"https://www.liacoleman.com/about\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">Lia Coleman</a>, <a href=\"https://piterpasma.nl/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">Piter Pasma</a>, <a href=\"https://ralenarc.com/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">RalenArc</a>, <a href=\"https://www.bartsimons.com/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">Bart Simons</a>, <a href=\"https://nicolevella.com/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">Nicole Vella</a>, <a href=\"https://ippsketch.com/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">ippsketch</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.melissawiederrecht.com/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">Melissa Wiederrecht</a> convened online, sharing code and processes through critiques and reviews. Selected from <a href=\"https://www.genartclub.com/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">The Generative Artists Club</a>, these creators operate as hybrid artist-engineers. Unlike the <a href=\"https://www.experimentsinartandtechnology.org/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"link\">E.A.T.</a> collaborations of the 1960s, this generation shares not just aesthetic critique, but the logic and debugging...",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/8ac46597-89fe-42bd-091d-164c98578700/raw"
    },
    "the-experiment-6jy": {
      "title": "The Experiment",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Casey REAS, Addie Wagenknecht, Rick Silva, and exonemo",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x7c7ba8a0dda1b96f9a3714e02d3cf9fa642849b3",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Casey%20REAS%2C%20Addie%20Wagenknecht%2C%20Rick%20Silva%2C%20and%20exonemo"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "In 2019, I invited Rick Silva, Addie Wagenknecht, and exonemo to launch a2p (artist-to-peer network), an experiment in decentralized exchange. Created by artists for artists, a2p explored digital art through direct, non-financial trading. Participants swapped editions of new works, building collections outside traditional market structures. Drawing inspiration from net.art and Fluxus, we engaged with the unique nature of digital media—where perfect, identical copies allow for global distribution without an \"original.\"\n\nThough we didn't use the term at the time, a2p works were NFTs registered on the Bitmark blockchain. As Bitmark retires its chain, we are migrating these works to Ethereum. To mark this transition, we have curated a selection of artists from the original 2019 exhibition for a new show on Feral File:\n\n<p class=\"m-l-56 display-inline-block\">Alan Butler, Andrew Benson, Auriea Harvey XLVIII, Émilie Brout & Maxime Marion, hiroyuki nisougi, Kim Asendorf, LaTurbo Avedon, Lee Blalock (L[3]^2), LIA, Morehshin Allahyari, Nicolas Sassoon, peter burr, Sabrina Ratté, Sara Ludy, Sarah Rara, shawné michaelain holloway, Tabor Robak, Theo Triantafyllidis, Yoshi Sodeoka</p>\n\nFollowing the 2019 launch, a2p 2.0 (2020) expanded to include a broader range of artists from China and South America. These experiments served as the foundation for Feral File, which I co-founded in 2020. While a2p relied on the social complexity of peer-to-peer trading, Feral File evolved this model: artists now trade with every other participant, and larger edition sizes allow collectors to join in and financially support the work.\n\na2p was a moment of collaboration before the rapid shifts that defined the subsequent history of digital art. It remains the essential precursor to what we are building today.\n\n— Casey Reas, 21 May 2023\n\n<hr>\n<p class=\"font-14\"><sup>1</sup>Except for the artists who requested their work not be migrated to Ethereum.</p>",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/8ba40bce-1112-4321-d071-d6b26ba8ae00/raw"
    },
    "in-medias-res-u9p": {
      "title": "In Medias Res",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "FEMMEBIT",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x8d887bb1ceb78472f5fa9c61333e172cd4c0e595",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/FEMMEBIT"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "*In Medias Res* celebrates feminist and post-cyberfeminist artists based in Los Angeles, exploring digital uprootedness and the liminal spaces of belonging. Taking its name from the narrative device meaning “into the midst of things,” the exhibition invites viewers to engage with the heart of nine artists’ stories as they navigate daily life in relation to Hollywood and society at large.\n\nThe exhibition also comments on the nature of NFTs—media without a beginning, middle, or end. As looping moving images, these works exist in a post-time, post-narrative state, contrasting with the conventions of traditional film. Emerging from FEMMEBIT, a grassroots movement founded in 2016 to showcase female and non-binary voices in digital futures, this project reinterprets Los Angeles through personal, queer, and alternative histories, challenging the city’s hegemonic, heteronormative \"Barbie\"-esque ideations.\n\nThe artists—working across film, digital art, and internet culture—have collectively created 33 pieces. In numerology, 33 signifies artistic expression, creativity, and the importance of community. This collaborative spirit defines FEMMEBIT, reflecting the artists' navigation of LA’s diverse creative industries, from analog video and animation to AI, game engines, and XR. \n\nWhile NFTs remain a contentious subject, FEMMEBIT views them as a vital intersection for supporting womxn, femmes, and non-binary artists and exploring the representation of digital art. \n\nFEMMEBIT is fiscally sponsored by Fractured Atlas. Donations support this digital exhibition and our ongoing operations. To contribute, visit paypal.me/femmebit.\n\nSpecial thanks to our members: Kate Parsons, Janna Avner, Sharsten Plenge, Giselle Zatonyl, Julieta Gil, Eva Aguila, Dahn Gim, and Richelle Ellis, and to our extensive community of FEMMEBIT alumni.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/d187c832-0410-42d7-590a-3a40128fc300/raw"
    },
    "truth-gkx": {
      "title": "Truth",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Adam Berninger",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x4b8d0a803e0446a277a3ddd049d7e3ec2ce65def",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Adam%20Berninger"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "<p>Charles and Ray Eames’ <em>Powers of Ten</em> reveals formal similarities across scales, from the cosmic to the cellular, leaving us with the uneasy conclusion that these vast extremes are not so different. While scientific fields like Psychophysical Scaling attempt to quantify these perceptions, artists often embrace the unquantifiable, even when working with rigid algorithms.</p>\n<p>The exhibition <em>Truth</em> features generative artists—Tyler Boswell, Presstube + bzor, Ella Hoeppner, Charlotte Dann, David Seven, HAL09999, and Lisa Orth—who use code to explore their intuition and \"inner truth.\" By utilizing building blocks like cells, threads, light, and forces, these artists abstract the range of what is possible, obscuring the boundaries between the minute and the enormous to ultimately unite them.</p>\n<p><em>“Art is a lie that makes us realize the truth.” — Pablo Picasso</em></p>\n<p>Though these works appear cosmic, tactile, or microscopic, their abstraction hides their literal definitions. By viewing these series together, we embrace the difficulty of naming a singular \"truth.\" The varieties and commonalities within these works reflect the universe’s infinite self-similarity. By blurring scales and relationships, these artists pursue the greater intention of artmaking: truth-finding beyond definition.</p>",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/543365f8-93a3-4922-0d87-02fd25c95200/raw"
    },
    "sound-machines-xz1": {
      "title": "SOUND MACHINES",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "MoMA",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x3f2496e9871744b8e865835639cbb7f1063ddfde",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/MoMA"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "\"Music is like the wind. You don't know where it came from and you don't know where it went.” – *Eric Dolphy*\n\nSound has no center; it wraps around us, fleeting and immersive. *SOUND MACHINES* explores this strange world through seven artists whose work bridges optical and aural domains, creating new interfaces between sound, technology, and art.\n\nHolly Herndon and Mat Dryhurst’s *Play From Memory* (2024) compares human musical learning to machine training. Yoko Ono’s *SOUND PIECE V* (1996/2024) transforms a musical score into a digital, on-chain archive of laughter. Danielle Brathwaite-Shirley’s *CANCEL YOURSELF* (2024) is a multisensory video game that upends the roles of player and played. American Artist and Tommy Martinez’s *Integrity Protocol/Lower Limb Lecture* (2023/24) invites participants to devise soundscapes from synthesized drones, while 0xDEAFBEEF’s *PAYPHONE* (2024) uses live call-and-response to explore histories of telephony and tokenization.\n\nThese works build on a legacy where sound serves as an arena for perception. From the 1950s onward, electronic technologies expanded music to encompass vibrations and noise, shifting audiences from passive listeners to active participants in a world of constant signals. As John Cage noted, “There is no such thing as silence.”\n\nMoMA has long been a site for these crossings: from Cage’s 1943 concert and the 1969 debut of the Moog synthesizer to Sonny Rollins’s 1965 Sculpture Garden performance. Artists like Terry Adkins, Laurie Anderson, and Kraftwerk have long fused these disciplines. Today, the artists in *SOUND MACHINES* continue this history, redefining the sonic landscape through generative AI and blockchain. They come in like music, and go where we haven’t gone before.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/a50b0614-d85e-43d0-cff0-0979f1bcc100/raw"
    },
    "one-to-zero-bti": {
      "title": "One to Zero",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Ryan Kuo and Viktor Timofeev",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x546bb6bedcd0291e120b772d14e6be10d358b60a",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Ryan%20Kuo%20and%20Viktor%20Timofeev"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "<p>The exhibition title <em>One to Zero</em> reverses the tech-startup concept of \"zero to one,\" where entrepreneurs monetize the future. Following the bursting of that bubble, this show proposes slowing down—or stopping—at the brink of a theoretical high. To take a <em>deflationary</em> approach is to manifest the contradiction of making digital art today: balancing technical literacy with the pressure to find money hidden in the medium.</p>\n<p>The artists in <em>One to Zero</em> share conceptual interests and ways of working, with each series incremented 26 times—an alphabet-like system where parts are indivisible. Mira Dayal's <em>reCAPTCHA.otf</em> (2024) remakes Google's Turing test as a typeface that scrambles text, while Viktor Timofeev's <em>Human Abecedary: Inter-Letters</em> (2024) breaks the alphabet into a permanently foreign writing system. Damon Zucconi's <em>In Tongues</em> (2024) renders Proust as a babbling stream of letters, and Laura Brown's <em>Mon Alphabet en Signes</em> (2024) uses AI to narrate wooden blocks, straining to make meaning.</p>\n<p>Adriana Ramić's <em>Memory Intensified by Hand of Manual World</em> (2021/2024) presents animal-object hybrids that defy image-recognition systems. Ryan Kuo's <em>Downtime</em> (2024) mimics a business application crashing and recovering, while Laurel Schwulst and Sydney Shen's <em>Perfume Area: The Alphabet</em> (2024) bypasses quantitative systems with perfume reviews that shift like a breeze. Finally, Dirk Paesmans' <em>PURCHASE CANCELLED</em> (2024) captures error loops in a blockchain interface, halting transactions with the demand: <em>Where's your wallet?</em></p>\n<p>Each work employs a mechanism—crossing symbolic, computational, and natural systems—whose output remains legible even as its viability to the \"money machine\" is unclear. Together, these works spell out an unspoken rule: digital art today must answer to multiple callers, using means that can scarcely address their full meaning.</p>",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/1e1c3003-45be-4c09-a67c-c21599d15500/raw"
    },
    "crystalline-work-5ze": {
      "title": "crystalline work",
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "<p>data as flow data as light data as prism</p>\n<p>an ice (re)generation algorithm upon the poles</p>\n<p>a flux across the solar year</p>\n<p>they build</p>\n<p>patterns systems languages</p>\n<p>a glide \na drop \na work</p>\n<p>techne for a s&#39;oiled earth</p>\n<p>prismatic</p>\n<p>j</p>\n",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/aad4e4e7-5513-42e4-fbaa-1d3624e00800/raw"
    },
    "crawl-yzx": {
      "title": "CRAWL",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Casey REAS",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x457ee5f723c7606c12a7264b52e285906f91eea6",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Casey%20REAS"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "Travess Smalley’s <em>CRAWL</em> is an interactive, pixelated exploration of a shifting, labyrinthine map. Whether viewed as an autonomous performance or navigated via keyboard, the work oscillates between observation and investigation. It is not a game, yet it operates with rules and mechanics that evoke the lineage of <em>Rogue</em> and <em>NetHack</em>, while drawing deeper inspiration from artists like John F. Simon Jr., Harold Cohen, and Terry Winters, as well as natural phenomena like silk-spinning worms and superorganisms.\n\n<em>CRAWL</em> consists of 512 unique artworks generated by a Python-code system. Each “level” features distinct patterns and textures; as viewers navigate, specific colors trigger events that reveal new sections of the map. Collectors can “merge” multiple levels into a single, interconnected artwork, using embedded icons to traverse between them.\n\nThe series is complemented by <em>CRAWL WOODBLOCK SCREENSHOTS</em>, a collection of unique relief prints. Here, digital pixels are thresholded into black-and-white values and carved into wood. By using each block to pull only one print, Smalley creates a singular object that bridges the gap between code, digital fabrication, and the history of image-reproduction technology.\n\nBuilding on themes from his <em>Pixel Rugs</em> series and recent solo exhibitions, <em>CRAWL</em> further refines Smalley’s inquiry into digital-image generation. It is a project that simultaneously pushes the boundaries of how images are processed, understood, and physically experienced.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/9ec375b1-18c0-4d58-5f6d-a94ef14ab200/raw"
    },
    "liu-renopatan-patterns-of-flow-nhk": {
      "title": "流れのパターン / Patterns of Flow",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Yusuke Shono and Alex Estorick",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x73f559388e4d64069b1790980fb2d70c249bd53d",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Yusuke%20Shono%20and%20Alex%20Estorick"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "*Patterns of Flow* explores the legacy of philosopher Hiroshi Kawano (1925–2012), a pioneer of computer art in Japan. Kawano’s \"K-System\"—a framework of input, generative processing, and output—challenged Western notions of art as a strictly human domain, proposing instead an \"artistic simulation\" where the computer thinks freely. By returning to the source of Kawano’s experiments, this exhibition charts an alternative path for contemporary generative art.\n\nThe exhibition features works by Satoshi Aizawa, Saeko Ehara, Misaki Nakano, mole^3, Okazz, Senbaku, Shunsuke Takawo, Kaoru Tanaka, Kazuhiro Tanimoto, and ykxotkx. These artists engage with Kawano’s creative imaginary through a shared love of code as a medium. Central to this dialogue is Shunsuke Takawo, whose practice of \"daily coding\" mirrors Kawano’s vision of improvisational, recursive creation. By embracing the vertical format—reminiscent of both Kawano’s *Simulated Color Mosaic* and traditional *kakejiku* (hanging scrolls)—the participants treat daily outputs as units of a larger, evolving network. As Kawano described in *The Emergence of Network Aesthetics*, this process of \"synthesis from below\" allows a dynamic, pluralistic beauty to emerge from autonomous actions.\n\n*Patterns of Flow* is Feral File’s first hybrid exhibition, presented online and at NEORT++ in Tokyo (September 25 – October 6, 2024). It features contemporary works alongside historical pieces by Hiroshi Kawano, courtesy of Tama Art University Museum, and includes a special essay by Jasia Reichardt. The exhibition is supported by Noriaki Nakata and NEORT++, with research by Hasaqui Yamanobe.",
      "coverImage": "https://imagedelivery.net/iCRs13uicXIPOWrnuHbaKA/63b126b8-277c-4088-7f30-fdc5eee7f000/raw"
    },
    "e-volved-formulae-8an": {
      "title": "E-volved Formulae",
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
