    },
    "net-evil-das": {
      "title": "Net Evil",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Mackenzie Davenport",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x2f66e6ab6d1fa0dcd37dedd72293372ba4d63063",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Mackenzie%20Davenport"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "<p>In the 1990s, the internet was met with wonder, yet early warnings of cybercrime and misinformation lurked beneath the surface. Today, as the web integrates into every facet of life, its power to cause harm has grown alongside its utility. <em>Net Evil</em> examines this tension, framing the internet through the lens of the seven deadly sins—a moral framework established by Pope Gregory I in 590 to categorize human indecency.</p>\n<p>In this exhibition, seven artists explore how ancient vices manifest in our digital age:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lust:</strong> Ann Hirsch’s <em>A(nn)i’s Phantom Obsessions</em> uses an AI version of her younger self to create collages of imaginary crushes, constrained by LLM limitations.</li>\n<li><strong>Gluttony:</strong> Dadabots’ <em>Phat Phonk</em> is a resource-intensive mixtape where audio fragments link to downloads requiring up to 29TB of storage.</li>\n<li><strong>Greed:</strong> Steve Pikelny’s <em>Money Vortex</em> uses generative code to broadcast affirmations of wealth, mimicking online abundance videos.</li>\n<li><strong>Sloth:</strong> Damjanski’s <em>Napster 24</em> catalogs a fractured sleep schedule induced by internet use via data from 24 naps in 24 hours.</li>\n<li><strong>Wrath:</strong> Lorna Mills’ <em>Diatriber</em> presents a graphic display of simulated violence, utilizing ballistic dummies to visualize digital destruction.</li>\n<li><strong>Envy:</strong> Maya Man’s <em>Hard Copy</em> examines the “Sad Beige Lawsuit,” using legal diagrams to visualize mimicry in influencer culture.</li>\n<li><strong>Pride:</strong> SHL0MS’ <em>𝚅𝙰𝙽𝙸𝚃𝙸𝙴𝚂</em> uses celebrity mirror selfies to equate digital popularity with artistic value, stripping away subjects to reveal platform vanity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The internet is a mirror reflecting human nature. <em>Net Evil</em> invites us to see the web as an extension of ourselves, where ancient impulses are amplified and broadcast to billions.</p>",
      "coverImage": "https://cdn.feralfileassets.com/exhibition-thumbnails/96a205cc-3e5c-46a7-9662-478dbec8209d/1755593015"
    },
    "console-spirituality-m6j": {
      "title": "Console Spirituality",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "LAN Party",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0xf0e06b9d8f48454ae44be7b43a408c944d58f1d1",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/LAN%20Party"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "<p>Video games offer more than entertainment; through worldbuilding and camaraderie, they foster conditions that transcend lived reality. <em>Console Spirituality</em> explores this transition, where virtual realms provide speculative visions for alternative ways of existing. By subverting hegemonic systems and experimenting with game logic, five artists render new deities and divine visions of virtual worldbuilding.</p>\n<p>Structured as a quest, the exhibition unfolds in four levels: the underground maze of <em>Dungeoneer</em>, the drifting plains of <em>Data Pilgrims</em>, the fractured heavens of <em>Angelcores &amp; Heavenly Sprites</em>, and the transcendent <em>Omoiyari</em>.</p>\n<p>John Provencher’s <em>Dungeoneer</em> uses generative <em>Doom</em>-inspired maps to challenge traditional exhibition-making and the boundaries of viewership. Emi Kusano’s <em>Data Pilgrims</em> utilizes AI to create meditative, ritualistic journeys that echo the slow exploration of “walking simulators,” finding spirituality in algorithmic glitches. Sabato Visconti’s <em>Angelcores &amp; Heavenly Sprites</em> engages with glitch art and modding history, scrambling retro game dialogue to queer technology and critique hegemony. Finally, Keiken and Gabriel Massan’s <em>Omoiyari</em> presents AR posters that subvert commercial aesthetics, using the Japanese concept of <em>omoiyari</em> (compassion) to reflect on memory, care, and tenderness.</p>\n<p>Together, these works defy reality to evangelize a speculative way of being. By implicating viewers as active agents within their digital landscapes, the artists invite us to contemplate our own virtual nirvana, proving that video games are powerful vessels for deep, emotional, and spiritual connection.</p>",
      "coverImage": "https://cdn.feralfileassets.com/exhibition-thumbnails/4225a453-c4d6-474e-be36-e0e15d0f1595/1750084695"
    },
    "star-quest-adm": {
      "title": "StarQuest",
      "curators": [
        {
          "name": "Nora O’ Murchú",
          "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x8f3db771cf012e2a81b1a8915c287a5e7a30056b",
          "url": "https://feralfile.com/curators/Nora%20O%E2%80%99%20Murch%C3%BA"
        }
      ],
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "<p>We live inside images that no longer merely represent reality but actively construct it. In this environment, visibility is a form of labor—a perpetual performance optimized by systems that filter and route our behavior. Maya Man’s <em>StarQuest</em> explores this terrain by mapping the disciplined world of competitive dance onto the logic of generative AI.</p>\n\n<p>Competition dance, with its emphasis on precision, repetition, and the constant obligation to be watched, mirrors the demands of contemporary digital life. Man treats this atmosphere as material, translating the grammar of dance into a dataset. Through generative AI, she constructs the “AI default girl”—an artifact of training data optimized to perform charm and composure. She is not a character, but a computational output, her movements inherited from a library of extracted gestures.</p>\n\n<p><em>StarQuest</em> presents 111 AI-generated dance scenes, each eight seconds long with synthetic music and lyrics. These clips function as building blocks in a system that exposes how visibility has become a learned pattern. By echoing the ethos of series like <em>Dance Moms</em>, Man highlights the uncanny alignment between human rehearsal and algorithmic training: both demand compliance in exchange for recognition.</p>\n\n<p>The work sits at the intersection of autobiography and automation, drawing on Man’s own history in competitive dance. By reducing choreography to computation and the dancer to an interface, <em>StarQuest</em> reveals the mechanics of an image culture that values instant, fleeting recognition over connection. Man does not stage the failure of AI to imitate dance; instead, she exposes the conditions under which images now circulate—as fragments and signals optimized for processing. In doing so, she gives form to a reality where images no longer mirror the world, but silently govern it.</p>",
      "coverImage": "https://cdn.feralfileassets.com/exhibition-thumbnails/da0f0fce-1ff9-4550-ae72-b01755f0fbfa/1763349992"
    },
    "ex-nihilo-a3c": {
      "title": "Ex Nihilo",
      "publisher": {
        "name": "Feral File",
        "key": "did:pkh:eip155:1:0x1d05cf6c6beb0c869851bfdb9510d4e44e855ad6",
        "url": "https://feralfile.com"
      },
      "summary": "This is a new exhibition within the “Still Life” series of exhibitions. It’s my first solo exhibition with Feral File, an online gallery I co-founded in 2020. The Still Life exhibitions started in 2016 with There’s No Distance at the bitforms gallery in New York and the most recent was Purely Platonic at DAM Project in Berlin in Summer 2025. This series of work utilizes the form and ideas surrounding the five Platonic Solid. The show in Berlin focused on Cube → EARTH and the Feral File work is grounded in Dodecahedron → COSMOS.",
      "coverImage": "https://cdn.feralfileassets.com/exhibition-thumbnails/2b290b34-46db-4b14-b411-9b30f68ae208/1772608129"
    }
  }
}
